WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.459 --> 00:00:05.420
It: Chapter Two has finally hit the big screen,
and while the epic conclusion to the adaptation

00:00:05.420 --> 00:00:11.519
of Stephen King's 1986 novel It has largely
left fans satisfied, a fair number of critics

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:12.520
weren't so impressed.

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:16.720
Fans of the original book and the films are
likely to shoot down a few of the pervasive

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:19.460
criticisms of the flick with these arguments.

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:24.579
Several changes were made to the character
of Derry Library resident Mike Hanlon and

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:27.320
criticisms around these changes can be confusing.

00:00:27.490 --> 00:00:31.860
All of the Losers are bullied, but in King's
novel, Henry Bowers' bullying of Mike was

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:35.400
particularly awful because of Bowers' unrestrained
racism.

00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:39.920
Some observers took issue with the fact that,
in both movies, the racist element the bullying

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:44.180
against Mike was substantially toned down,
leading to charges of squeamishness on the

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:47.690
part of director Andy Muschietti and screenwriter
Gary Dauberman.

00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:52.420
But the racist nature of the Bowers gang's
bullying of Mike is certainly implied, if

00:00:52.420 --> 00:00:54.240
no racial slurs are used on screen.

00:00:56.120 --> 00:00:57.440
"Stay the f--- out of my town!"

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:03.140
And it's worth remembering that the films
are set 31 years later than the novel, with

00:01:03.140 --> 00:01:06.960
Chapter One set in 1989 rather than 1958.

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:11.600
Racism is, of course, still pervasive today,
but it's not a huge stretch to say that the

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:15.960
use of overt slurs was more likely to be encountered
in the '50s than the '80s.

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:20.020
And neither film really suffered from the
filmmakers' decision to make the racial aspect

00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:22.640
of Bowers' antagonism toward Mike more subtle.

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:28.020
The decision was the obvious byproduct not
only of the time period switch, but of adapting

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:28.800
a 33-year old novel.

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:34.560
Another difference between literary Mike and
cinematic Mike is the fate of his parents;

00:01:34.579 --> 00:01:38.540
they were incorporated into Mike's tragic
backstory in the movies, having burned to

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:42.080
death inside a locked apartment while young
Mike watched helplessly.

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:47.140
A newspaper headline conspicuously shown in
It: Chapter Two refers to a pair of "crackheads"

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:51.130
who set themselves on fire in front of their
young son, but this is later revealed to be

00:01:51.130 --> 00:01:52.360
one of Pennywise's tricks.

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:57.280
After the malevolent entity is defeated, the
true headline is revealed, and it's made clear

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:00.020
that Mike's parents actually died in an electrical
fire.

00:02:00.149 --> 00:02:03.940
The reason for this change is fairly obvious:
For narrative reasons, the poor kid needed

00:02:03.950 --> 00:02:05.479
a good dose of trauma.

00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:09.440
And the fake headline plays off of the Bowers
gang's racially charged bullying.

00:02:09.690 --> 00:02:14.880
But one spectacularly wrongheaded review by
Slate's Jack Hamilton opined that the change

00:02:14.880 --> 00:02:16.540
itself smacked of racism.

00:02:16.580 --> 00:02:17.080
He wrote:

00:02:17.320 --> 00:02:21.760
"To transform the only black protagonist from
the child of responsible, nurturing parents

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:26.431
into the child of negligent crack cocaine
addicts is far worse than lazy writing; it's

00:02:26.431 --> 00:02:30.310
to actively draw from a deeply racist set
of cultural tropes."

00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:34.250
We guess he must have been yawning really
wide or looking at his watch when the true

00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:35.770
newspaper headline was revealed.

00:02:36.770 --> 00:02:41.150
Of all the critical gripes leveled at It:
Chapter Two, by far the most common had to

00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:43.900
do with the film's nearly three-hour run time.

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:46.980
The film was labeled "arduous," "muddled,"
and "meandering."

00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:47.980
"Why?

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:49.960
What is taking so long?

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:54.980
I wasted all day here for you!"

00:02:55.820 --> 00:03:00.660
Of course, King's novel is more than 1,100
pages long, and a huge amount of material

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:03.380
was left out in bringing the story to the
screen.

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:07.520
Some elements were combined into details in
the films' story, such as the fire that killed

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:11.930
Mike's parents being a clear reference to
the novel's fire at the Black Spot, a historical

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:16.080
event which used to illustrate Pennywise's
centuries-long influence on Derry.

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:20.920
Those going into It: Chapter Two expecting
a brisk, breezily plotted ride to the final

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:25.040
showdown either weren't familiar with the
source material, or were expecting Dauberman

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:28.180
and Muschietti to take far more liberties
than they did.

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:32.600
The individual and collective journeys of
the Losers Club play directly into how they

00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:37.131
deal with the horror of that final confrontation,
and if they had been given short shrift, it's

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:40.620
likely that many of these same critics would
have been complaining about that.

00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:45.620
Many of It: Chapter Two's critics also took
issue with its humorous tone, in particular

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:49.680
the wisecracks the adult members of the Losers
Club are prone to lobbing at each other as

00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:54.570
they prepare to face off against a horror
so unimaginable that they almost all forgot

00:03:54.570 --> 00:03:55.920
their first encounter with It.

00:03:56.080 --> 00:04:00.680
Much of the humor is even intertwined with
the horror, as with a brilliant visual homage

00:04:00.680 --> 00:04:03.700
to John Carpenter's 1982 classic The Thing.

00:04:04.120 --> 00:04:08.010
But it's clear for some of the characters,
the humor is there to mask the fear.

00:04:08.010 --> 00:04:12.070
It's the old "whistling past the graveyard"
trope; one laughs so that they may not soil

00:04:12.070 --> 00:04:13.920
themselves out of sheer terror.

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:18.090
The Losers Club is an irreverent bunch in
both the novel and the films, and the banter

00:04:18.090 --> 00:04:21.939
doesn't just have the effect of entertaining
the audience, it also keeps them from going

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:22.440
insane.

00:04:22.700 --> 00:04:25.480
If anything, the humor is even dialed down
from the book.

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:30.320
Perhaps the most frustrating reviews of It:
Chapter Two were those that completely whiffed

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:32.110
in pinpointing the film's themes.

00:04:32.110 --> 00:04:36.949
Take, for example, this excerpt from the review
of NBC News' Noah Berlatsky:

00:04:36.949 --> 00:04:41.210
"Chapter Two presents itself as an exploration
of nostalgia and memory.

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:44.870
The adults magically forget their childhood
adventures when they move away from Derry,

00:04:44.870 --> 00:04:48.560
and the movie is about them reacquainting
themselves with their past friendships and

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:50.680
with their true selves upon their return."

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:55.319
What the Losers are reacquainting themselves
with, voluntarily or not, is the horror of

00:04:55.319 --> 00:04:59.800
their youth - the searing, inescapable trauma
which many of us deal with, and which is often

00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:03.580
buried not by some magic, but as a form of
emotional self-defense.

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:05.970
Dauberman spelled this out in an interview
with Inverse.

00:05:05.970 --> 00:05:10.120
"I think as an adult, we all have some repressed
trauma somewhere in us.

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:12.770
I'm the same age as the Losers in [It: Chapter
Two].

00:05:12.770 --> 00:05:17.199
I followed up on my own childhood, and how
easy it is for memories to fade.

00:05:17.199 --> 00:05:18.599
Even the more traumatic ones.

00:05:18.599 --> 00:05:21.320
Like getting details wrong, or [asking], 'Did
that happen?

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:21.820
When?

00:05:21.820 --> 00:05:22.520
I can't remember.'"

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:24.320
The biggest bully of all

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:27.039
The esoteric ending of King's novel is practically
unfilmable.

00:05:27.039 --> 00:05:31.479
The filmmakers behind It: Chapter Two offered
up their own interpretation, and of course,

00:05:31.479 --> 00:05:36.280
many critics jumped all over it: As a monstrous
Pennywise menaces them, the Losers essentially

00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:40.389
take away his power by ridiculing him until
he's physically small enough that they can

00:05:40.389 --> 00:05:43.039
rip out and destroy his still-beating heart.

00:05:43.039 --> 00:05:46.840
At least one observer came somewhat close
to the mark by stating that the Losers "sort

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:51.139
of bully [Pennywise] to death" during a conversation
with Muschietti, only for the director to

00:05:51.139 --> 00:05:52.139
set him straight:

00:05:52.139 --> 00:05:56.120
"I didn't see it as bullying so much as it
was about reversing the power of belief on

00:05:56.120 --> 00:06:00.729
this creature, who uses belief as a weapon
to kill his victims…For the first time,

00:06:00.729 --> 00:06:05.449
it's the Losers who have this power, and they
start telling him what he is…You're a headless

00:06:05.449 --> 00:06:06.949
boy; you're a weak old woman.

00:06:06.949 --> 00:06:11.129
And finally they converge on the concept of
a clown, which is just the most helpless and

00:06:11.129 --> 00:06:16.490
powerless thing…The power of belief is Pennywise's
power, but it's also his biggest weakness."

00:06:16.490 --> 00:06:20.860
So it is with all bullies, whose victims must
feel weak for them to feel powerful.

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:23.460
Pennywise is simply the biggest bully of them
all.

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:27.419
So once that "power of belief" is taken away,
he has nothing left.

00:06:27.419 --> 00:06:28.580
"Losers stick together."

